ve every loss/equation with the meaning of each term, say why each component exists, and refer to the architecture figure in the text.\n- Experiments: setup (data, baselines, metrics, protocol) before results; every table and figure is referenced in the text, and the text states what the reader should conclude from it (the comparison, the size of the gap, the cause). Include ablations and analysis supplied in the material; interpret them, do not just restate the numbers.\n- Limitations: a real top-level \\section{Limitations} when the task lists it as a section.\n- Conclusion: be candid about what the material does not establish; no new claims.\n- Write in specific, plain academic prose. No filler (\"in recent years, X has attracted great attention\"), no unexplained superlatives, no repeated sentences across sections.\n\n## Truthfulness\n- Every number, dataset name, method name and claim must come from the supplied material. Never invent results, hyperparameters, seeds, variances or details that the material does not give; when something needed is absent, say what is unreported instead of making it up, or leave it out.\n- Numbers in the abstract/introduction must match the tables exactly.\n\n## Citations (this matters most for quality)\n- Cite a work only where the sentence is about what that work does. Read the entry (title/abstract/venue) first; the claim you attach to it must be true of it.\n- Use the bibliography as your literature: a strong paper has dense, relevant citing, so go through the bibliography topic by topic and cite every entry that genuinely belongs in a sentence of the introduction, related work, method or experiments (baselines, datasets, backbones, tools used, prior findings you build on). Typically this is a large share of the bibliography, because it was assembled for this paper.\n- If the overview names a work that is absent from the bibliography, name it in the text without a \\cite and never invent a key.\n- Entries that do not fit any sentence you can honestly write stay uncited. Never dump keys: no `\\cite{a,b,c,d,e,f,...}` bundles of more than 4 keys, no \"see also\" lists, no appended citation paragraph, and no citing for the sake of coverage. A bundle is acceptable only when the sentence makes a claim that every key in it supports.\n- Prefer attaching a citation to a specific statement (\"X [cite] first showed that ...; Y [cite] extends this to ...\"), which is both more useful to the reader and checkable.\n- Use only keys that exist in the supplied .bib file; use the right command (\\citet for subject, \\citep for parenthetical, or \\cite if the style requires).\n\n## Format\n- Use the supplied template and style files exactly; do not alter margins or fonts. Target the page length the task states (the main text should fill it; do not pad with empty space or repeated content, and do not exceed it).\n- Sections required by the task must exist as real \\section commands. Figures and tables must come from the supplied files with informative captions that stand alone, and are referenced with \\ref.\n- Final files go exactly where the task says; manuscript.pdf must be compiled from the final manuscript.tex.\n\n## Self-check before finishing\n- [ ] Every supplied figure and table is included and discussed in the text.\n- [ ] Each section required by the task exists and is substantive.\n- [ ] No invented numbers or facts; abstract numbers equal table numbers.\n- [ ] Every \\cite key exists in the .bib; no \"??\" or undefined references in the PDF; no TODO text.\n- [ ] Each citation sits in a sentence that is about that work. No key bundles of more than 4. Skim the cite list: any entry I cited only to cite it should be removed or given a real sentence.\n- [ ] Related work is thematic and says how this paper differs.\n- [ ] Page count meets the task's target; the PDF was recompiled after the last edit.\n\n\n## Finish gate (run these commands before the final push; fix anything they flag, then recompile)